Dear Ines, Emeka and Connie,

Thank you, thank you, thank you! Yesterday was exactly the afternoon I'd
hoped for, and a big part of that was the three of you. I'm still a bit
buzzy from it, so forgive the exclamation points.

Quick numbers while I have them: 61 registered, 38 attended. For a
Thursday afternoon in midterm season I'll take that happily - and the
people who came stayed until the very end, which I can't say for every
event I've run in Pellery.

Ines - your keynote set the tone perfectly. "Evaluate the use, not the
tool" has already turned up in two emails in my inbox this morning, so
it's officially a catchphrase now. And thank you for carrying on so
gracefully when the projector gave up right at your results slide.

Connie - your point on the panel that satisfaction is not learning was
the moment of the afternoon for me. You could feel the room shift. I
heard it quoted at the coffee table afterwards more than once (with
approval, I promise!).

Emeka - thank you for the energy, the CHEM 110 stories and for backing
Connie up from the floor. You made "try it on your own syllabus" feel
doable for a lot of nervous people.

One lovely surprise: nine faculty asked about some kind of community of
practice to keep the conversation going - it came up in the last Q&A
and again over the cookies. Emeka, I saw you put your hand up to
co-lead straight away! I'll be in touch once I've caught my breath.

The feedback survey responses are still coming in - I'll share them
later today once the stragglers have had a chance to fill it in.

